# TODO - Shared internationalisation and localisation

## Objective

Make all games and apps localisable through one shared mechanism without
coupling gameplay, assets, platform adapters or text to a single language.

## Acceptance

- Extend the existing engine translation catalogue rather than creating
  parallel title-specific implementations. Use stable message identifiers,
  admitted BCP 47 locale tags and explicit regional/language fallback.
  Each title supplies its default and fallback. Keep policy portable.
- Each title declares its supported locales and required translated
  surfaces, including shared UI and unique messages. Do not impose a
  global language list. Admit additional languages without changing
  engine code; report missing keys and invalid catalogues.
- Localise user-facing menus, settings, HUD, prompts, controls, errors and
  applicable subtitles. Do not translate internal identifiers, save keys,
  protocols, gameplay rules or original asset identities. Preserve the
  original text and artwork as an explicit option when appropriate.
- Provide a language selector in shared settings, persist the selection
  in the existing preference contract, restore it on launch and use
  deterministic fallback for unavailable or invalid locales. Host locale
  discovery, if used, belongs to the platform IR, not gameplay.
- Support Unicode text and licensed glyph coverage, including accents,
  inverted punctuation and other locale scripts. Measure text by rendered
  glyphs rather than UTF-8 bytes; verify wrapping and adaptive layout.
  Evaluate existing shaping, bidirectional and pluralisation mechanisms
  before introducing new ones; test them for languages that require them.
- Use bounded catalogue loading and typed message parameters, not
  executable translation content. Reject malformed inputs and preserve
  identical gameplay, input routing and save semantics across languages.
- Demonstrate the first title in its declared locales on Linux and the
  supported browser path. Verify a real language change with at least
  two catalogue selections, fallback, persistence, missing glyphs,
  longer labels and presentation modes. Use synthetic fixtures for
  additional scripts and languages not supported by the title.
- Keep authored message catalogues distinct from imported edition media.
  Language-dependent original text, fonts, voice and artwork enter via
  the existing importer IR and canonical asset namespace where applicable.
  Use typed locale/resource metadata and shared content identities; never
  duplicate whole asset trees per language or target. Do not require
  localised media that the selected lawful edition does not contain.
- Require every future title and app to reuse the shared localisation
  mechanism and verify its own complete supported-language catalogues.

## Evidence

The completed input and presentation contract introduced a portable
TranslationCatalog with exact-locale, primary-language and configured
fallback lookup. Its existing synthetic test covers en, es, es-MX,
es-ES, an unsupported locale and an unknown message key. Review on
2026-11-26 found that shared menu labels remain hardcoded in English
and the compact bitmap text renderer measures UTF-8 byte length and
draws ASCII glyphs. Language selection, persistent locale preferences,
Unicode layout and integrated title translations are not implemented.

Scope clarification, 2026-11-26: supported languages and the required
localisation surfaces belong to each title, not the shared engine.
The initial English and Mexican Spanish example is not a global
mandate. Simpsons Arcade is the first integration and must demonstrate
its declared supported locales before the next catalogue title begins.
The shared importer remains the sole admission path for localised
original media; authored messages may be bundled as authored data.

Shared infrastructure progress, 2026-11-26:

- `88b5429`: compact Unicode bitmap glyphs, accent composition and visible
  replacement coverage for unsupported characters. This is not general
  script shaping or bidirectional layout.
- `fe07b01`: bounded locale and catalogue admission, atomic versioned
  UTF-8 loading, exact completeness checks, deterministic fallback and
  typed named parameters with explicit output budgets. Locale admission
  covers language, script, region and variant subtags; extensions, private
  use and registry alias canonicalisation are not yet admitted.
- `13a7db9`: shared menu keys, optional English and Mexican Spanish
  resources, catalogue-driven headings and rows, typed settings values.
- `ff33ba9`: shared control editor catalogue with translated static and
  dynamic labels, including scale reduction for long labels.
- `10bb423`: canonical locale preference codec and atomic locale store
  using the existing shared persistence contract.
- `5f18af3`: title-configured optional language selection, deterministic
  index cycling, exact menu atlas accounting and a seven-row settings
  layout test.

All above changes passed `cargo validate`, including the final fresh
commit-hook run and WebAssembly smoke builds.

Title integration, 2026-11-26:

- `87c31ed`: Simpsons Arcade declares English (`en`, default) and
  Mexican Spanish (`es-MX`). The native and browser runtimes select
  resident menu and control-editor art, publish locale changes via the
  shared store and restore the setting. Unsupported stored tags fall
  back without rewriting the stored value; malformed preferences remain
  untouched and language selection becomes session-only.
- `8c06683`: the title localisation diagnostic uses procedural assets,
  the actual settings navigation and renderer readback. It checks
  visible language changes, exact restoration on cycling, an unsupported
  locale, persistence on reopening, unchanged unrelated preferences and
  all five presentation policies. The Linux opt-in render gate and
  Firefox WebAssembly execution passed.
- `ef11219`: authored station, remote availability, join/rejoin countdown,
  and compact control prompts use the same selected catalogue, with
  bounded per-locale atlas accounting and unchanged character identity.
  The native render gate and repeated Firefox execution passed.
- `66d2435`: standalone controller hints, caption and runtime transport
  recovery messages use the locale from its admitted settings directory.
  Multi-seat touch labels are localised, with identical button pixels and
  hit geometry; a single unlabelled panel stays shared. Physical binding
  identities and LAN protocol identifiers remain unchanged. The fresh
  native render gate and commit-hook validation passed.

The subsequent two-seat procedural diagnostic verifies that switching
languages changes the rendered bottom touch strip and cycling restores
the entire frame. The native render gate passed with this fixture; the
Firefox execution reported both localisation and title smoke success,
49 delivered callbacks, 55 blits and clean canvas removal. Native and
browser diagnostics use isolated preference locations.

### Localisation acceptance progress, 2026-11-26

- `d036c2e`: native and browser startup messages, directory chooser
  prompts, terminal outcomes and diagnostics use the selected catalogue.
  Underlying diagnostic detail remains intact. The package shell preserves
  a Rust diagnostic and uses a generic fallback only before Rust starts.
- `3636b70`: compact bitmap glyph measurement admits the exact maximum
  representable extent. Glyph coverage reporting and bounded wrapping
  preserve decomposed supported accents, reject insufficient geometry,
  and identify unsupported scripts. Both declared title catalogues pass
  glyph coverage checks.
- `b33ed9b`: shared menu hints that cannot fit a single line use the
  bounded glyph-aware wrapping mechanism. The regression covers rendered
  atlas allocation, following-hint placement and insufficient height.

All three commits passed fresh validation and fresh commit-hook checks.
The asset-free browser package was checked at 88 files, 1,695,117 bytes;
receipt 3ce159fa51bd85bcdc1aa7e28b67990eb49c4a0aaaeb2577e4874cb45b9e3f97.
Seven actual Firefox startup cases passed: English and restored Spanish
prompt/label; English and Spanish unsupported picker capability; missing
JavaScript and missing WebAssembly; and corrupt persisted locale before
selection. The Spanish fixture was written by the Rust diagnostic.
Each verified the status, picker presence or absence, absence of a
canvas and unchanged saved locale. Two additional cases exercised trusted
chooser cancellation in English and Spanish in isolated Xvfb and
Bubblewrap environments. The picker was removed and no preference was
overwritten. The invalid-directory chooser harness timed out and is not
a passing case. These tests are not physical-device acceptance.

The task remains open. Remaining scope includes localised original-media
resource metadata where applicable and the assessment of additional
script coverage, shaping, bidirectional text and pluralisation before
admitting languages that require them. The procedural readbacks do not
establish physical touchscreen/controller acceptance, original-edition
gameplay parity or broader browser support.
